<body>
<h1>Unit 2: Vectors</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Vectors are the bookkeeping that lets a physical law stay true no matter how you choose to look at the situation. The unit is about representation: when a scalar law becomes a vector law, and which errors in representation produce wrong answers that still look plausible.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>A vector needs both magnitude and direction; two vectors of equal magnitude pointing in different directions are different vectors, unlike scalars.</li><li>Two vectors are equal only when they have identical magnitude and direction, regardless of the path taken to produce them, which is the formal statement of the principle of transmissibility.</li><li>Components are projections onto a chosen orthonormal frame; the frame is free to choose, but the numerical answer changes with the choice even though the physics does not.</li><li>The dot product A.B = AB cos(theta) is a scalar measuring alignment; the cross product magnitude |A x B| = AB sin(theta) is a scalar measuring perpendicularity, and the vector A x B is axial (a pseudovector).</li><li>Displacement, velocity, acceleration, force and momentum are vectors; speed, energy, temperature, mass and power are scalars.</li><li>Vector addition is commutative and associative, so the order in which forces are added cannot change the result - only the arithmetic errors can.</li><li>A vector needs three independent components in three dimensions, so a two-dimensional diagram silently drops one - the component the page cannot show is exactly the one a cross product uses.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Component arithmetic is valid only inside a single orthonormal frame; mixing components taken from different frames produces a number with no meaning.</li><li>The cross product obeys the right-hand rule and is anti-commutative: A x B = -(B x A), so the order of the operands matters even though the magnitudes are equal.</li><li>Two drawn vectors of equal length that look perpendicular on paper are not perpendicular if the page is scaled unevenly; always check magnitude and direction numerically.</li><li>Adding forces that act on different bodies is a category error: Newton's third-law pairs cancel in the total but each acts on something else, so they cannot be merged into one resultant on one object.</li><li>The cross product is defined only in three dimensions, so the planar perpendicular you draw is a construction convention rather than a coordinate-independent operation.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 3 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Triangle, parallelogram and polygon laws of vectors</li><li>Resolution of vectors; unit vectors</li><li>Scalar and vector products</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Two vectors are equal if they have the same magnitude, whatever their direction.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Equality of vectors requires both magnitude and direction to match. Two equal-length vectors at 120 degrees to each other have a resultant of magnitude equal to each one, not zero - the classic trap in which the student 'adds' magnitudes and forgets direction.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> A = B only if |A| = |B| and the direction of A is the same as the direction of B. Two vectors of equal magnitude at 120 degrees have a resultant of the same magnitude as each, because 2 A cos(60 degrees) = A.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The dot product A.B of two vectors is another vector, like the cross product.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The dot product is a scalar: it is a single number with no direction, equal to |A||B| cos theta. Only the cross product is a vector, and it is perpendicular to both operands.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> A.B = |A||B| cos theta is a scalar, so A.B = B.A. A x B is a vector of magnitude |A||B| sin theta, perpendicular to the plane of A and B, and A x B = -(B x A).</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Multiplying a vector by a scalar changes its direction.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> A scalar multiple of a vector is parallel to it. The scalar sets the magnitude and may reverse the sense if it is negative, but the line of action is unchanged.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> k A is parallel to A for any real k; k &gt; 0 keeps the sense, k &lt; 0 reverses it, and k = 0 gives the zero vector, which has no defined direction.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A force of 10 N acts at 60 degrees to the horizontal. Find its horizontal and vertical components, and the magnitude of the resultant of this force and a 6 N force along the horizontal in the same sense.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Horizontal component = 10 cos 60 degrees = 5.0 N; vertical component = 10 sin 60 degrees = 8.66 N. The two forces are at 60 degrees, so the resultant is sqrt(5^2 + 6^2 + 2 x 5 x 6 cos 60 degrees) = sqrt(25 + 36 + 30) = sqrt(91) = 9.54 N. (Resolving first and then adding the components gives the same magnitude.)</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Two vectors P and Q have equal magnitude A and the angle between them is theta. Show that the resultant has the same magnitude as each vector when theta = 120 degrees.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The resultant of two equal vectors is R = 2 A cos(theta / 2), from the parallelogram law. Setting R = A gives 2 A cos(theta/2) = A, so cos(theta/2) = 1/2 and theta/2 = 60 degrees, hence theta = 120 degrees. At that angle the resultant is also along the bisector and has magnitude A.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A box of weight 50 N rests on a rough horizontal floor. A rope pulls it at 30 degrees above the horizontal. Explain which part of the pull actually tends to move the box, and what the upward part of the pull does to the contact force.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Only the horizontal component, T cos 30 degrees, tends to move the box; the vertical component T sin 30 degrees is perpendicular to the floor and merely reduces the normal reaction, so the friction available is mu (W - T sin 30 degrees) rather than mu W. The upward component therefore helps the box slide by reducing the normal force. This is the standard argument for why a rope pulled upward is less efficient than one pulled horizontally.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Degrees in a radian</td><td>180/pi = 57.2958</td></tr><tr><td>Resultant of two equal vectors at 120 degrees</td><td>equal to each one</td></tr><tr><td>Components of a unit vector at 30 degrees to the x-axis</td><td>0.866 and 0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Magnitude of the sum of two unit vectors at 90 degrees</td><td>sqrt(2) = 1.414</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does the quantum description of angular momentum look so different from the classical spinning-top picture?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Spin is not a literal rotation of a rigid body: it has no defined axis in the classical sense, yet behaves like an angular momentum with quantum numbers.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How does the quantum state of a spin-1/2 particle relate to the classical picture of a spinning top, if at all?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>The correspondence is suggestive but not exact: quantum angular momentum has no fixed axis, and the 1924 Stern-Gerlach experiment is still the cleanest experimental statement of the mismatch.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Resolve a 30 N force applied at 40 degrees to the horizontal into components, then find the resultant with a second 20 N force at 120 degrees.</li><li>Find the angle between two vectors of equal magnitude whose sum and difference happen to have the same magnitude, and interpret the result.</li><li>Verify a x (b x c) = b(a.c) - c(a.b) with components, then use it to find the rotation axis of a rigid body.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/vectors">Read the chapter notes: Vectors</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-vectors">Vector Addition 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-vectors-full">Vectors Comprehensive 3D lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/4-introduction-to-dynamics-newtons-laws-of-motion">OpenStax: College Physics 2e</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/2-1-scalars-and-vectors">OpenStax: Scalars and Vectors</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/2-3-algebra-of-vectors">OpenStax: Algebra of Vectors</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
